librevm (0.2.1) trixie; urgency=medium

  * Guest memory is now backed with large pages. Gunyah takes guest memory away
    from the host, and a page it takes cannot come back without being migrated
    out of CMA first; on a device whose 696 MiB CMA area has 40 MiB free, that
    migration has nowhere to go, and the failure mode reported elsewhere is a
    multi-minute host stall ending in an OOM kill, or a `qcom_scm` assignment
    failure that resets the machine. A 2 MiB page is one unit to hand over
    rather than 512. This matters more than it looks: guest memory is served
    from shmem, this kernel has `shmem_enabled = never`, so without the flag
    guest memory never used large pages at all. `[gpu] hugepages = false` turns
    it off.

  * The naming of a guest's CPU and GPU is gone. A device tree overlay had been
    setting `model`, which fixed fastfetch's `Host` line and nothing else,
    because fastfetch takes its `CPU` line from the device tree's `compatible`
    and splits the first entry on the comma. The image also carried a fastfetch
    config that listed only the two modules it renamed; a `modules` array is the
    whole list rather than a set of overrides, so a guest with that file printed
    two lines instead of the usual dozen. Both are removed.

  * Gunyah readiness accepts `gh_rm_drv` as well as the older three-module
    stack, so the check works on kernels that expose the resource manager under
    the newer name. Contributed by Goldzxcbug.

 -- librevm <noreply@github.com>  Thu, 15 Oct 2026 20:40:00 +0800

librevm (0.2.0) trixie; urgency=medium

  * A machine can be given a virtio-gpu, and its framebuffer appears in a window on
    the host. The three things that have to be right are documented: `displays=` rather
    than `width=`/`height=`, `--wayland-sock` as an argument rather than only in the
    environment, and running crosvm as root, without which Gunyah refuses the vcpu.
  * `librevm desktop install|start|stop|status|touch-area` puts XFCE in a guest and
    runs it. No xorg.conf: a configuration naming a driver makes X treat the GPU as a
    secondary device and find no screens. udev has to be running before X, or the
    virtio input devices exist and are invisible.
  * The window's keyboard and mouse are forwarded when a GPU is enabled; without them
    a desktop is a picture.
  * A touchscreen can be passed through, by librevm reading the panel and feeding
    crosvm over a socket, because the direct path takes the device exclusively and the
    desktop already holds it.
  * The guest's console is put on the framebuffer as well as the serial port, so the
    kernel log, the initramfs and the desktop all appear in the same window, as they do
    on a real machine.
  * The guest runs an init system. The boot initramfs used to hand over to a shell,
    which made services impossible to start at boot.
  * The hub keeps the pid file in place while crosvm lives, instead of writing it once
    and leaving nothing responsible for it.

 -- code002-2 <hiez832670@gmail.com>  Mon, 12 Oct 2026 10:30:00 +0000

librevm (0.1.0) trixie; urgency=medium

  * First release.
  * Virtual machine definitions in TOML, with validation that rejects the
    configurations Gunyah cannot accept.
  * A command line tool: create, list, show, plan, status, start, run, stop,
    console, delete.
  * A GTK4 window: sidebar, detail pane, console, and a create dialog.
  * A console that survives closing the window, served by a detached hub because
    crosvm's unix-stream serial is a client that needs someone to listen first.
  * English and Chinese, with a language menu and a remembered choice.
  * install-gunyah: builds the Gunyah modules from a kernel tree or installs
    prebuilt ones, verifying vermagic and branch-target support before touching
    /lib/modules.
  * The window checks for Gunyah on first start and offers to install it.

 -- code002-2 <hiez832670@gmail.com>  Sat, 11 Oct 2026 05:00:00 +0000
